 "permanova": {
  "title": "The same split, for a whole profile at once",
  "intro": "A crop can be judged by more than its weight. Dried and analyzed, a sample of tubers gives a list of constituents, and the question becomes whether variety and dung change the make-up of the crop as a whole, not one figure at a time. This board makes the same split as the yield board for two constituents at once: the tubers from each plot are a point placed by their nitrogen and their phosphoric acid, and the spread among the points is divided among variety, dung, their interaction, and the noise. Because figures like these seldom follow the normal curve, each part is tested by shuffling the labels instead of reading the F distribution. The method, permutational analysis of variance (PERMANOVA), was described by Marti Anderson in 2001, long after this station's trials. The figures are a small demonstration trial, not the station's own analyses.",
  "credit": "M. J. Anderson, \"A new method for non-parametric multivariate analysis of variance,\" Austral Ecology (2001)",
  "subtitle": "30 plots, each a point placed by its tubers' nitrogen and phosphoric acid. Press the numbered buttons in order to build the table.",
  "noun": "plots",
  "x": {
   "key": "nitrogen",
   "label": "nitrogen (% of dry matter)"
  },
  "y": {
   "key": "phosphoric_acid",
   "label": "phosphoric acid (% of dry matter)"
  },
